==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|bespeken|lang=enm}}, {{term|bispeken|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} *{{term||bespecan|lang=ang}}, {{term|besprecan||to speak about, speak against, accuse of, claim at law, complain|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*bisprekaną||to discuss, blame}}, equivalent to {{prefix|be|speak|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|bespeke||to beseech, speak or negotiate with|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|fy|-}} {{term|besprekke||to discuss|lang=fy}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|bespreken||to discuss, review, debate|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|besprechen||to discuss, review, talk about|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA| [bɪˈspiːk]|lang= en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|bespeaks|bespeaking|bespoke|past2=bespake|past2_qual=archaic|bespoken|past_ptc2=bespoke|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}

# {{label|en|transitive}} To [[speak]] about; tell of; [[relate]]; [[discuss]].
#* '''2006''', Janet Jaymes, ''Dirty Laundry: A Memoir'':
#*: But to '''bespeak''' of a love, heavily weighed upon a heart, toward someone opposing those sentiments encourages foolish and embarrassing repercussions he will never know about.
# {{label|en|transitive}} To speak for beforehand; [[engage]] in advance; make arrangements for; [[order]] or [[reserve]] [[in advance]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''bespeak''' a seat in a theatre}}
#* Sir {{w|Walter Scott}} (1771-1832)
#*: concluding, naturally, that to gratify his avarice was to '''bespeak''' his favour
#* {{quote-web|date=2012-08-01|author=Ed Yong|site=The Scientist|accessdate=2012-08-12
|title=[http://the-scientist.com/2012/08/01/replacement-parts/ Replacement Parts]
|passage=… others are attempting the more ambitious feat of engineering '''bespoke''' human organs from scratch.}}
# {{label|en|transitive}} To [[stipulate]], [[solicit]], [[ask for]], or [[request]], as in a favour.
#: {{ux|en|to '''bespeak''' a calm hearing}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''bespeak''' your patience in advance.}}
# {{label|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[forbode]]; [[foretell]].
# {{label|en|transitive|archaic|poetic}} To speak to; [[address]].
#* {{w|John Dryden}} (1631-1700)
#*: He thus the queen '''bespoke'''.
# {{label|en|transitive}} To [[betoken]]; [[show]]; [[indicate]]; [[foretell]]; [[suggest]].
#: {{ux|en|This act '''bespeaks''' his kindness.}}
#* {{w|Jonathan Swift}} (1667–1745)
#*: [They] '''bespoke''' dangers{{...}}in order to scare the allies.
#* {{w|John Locke}} (1632-1705)
#*: When the abbot of St. Martin was born, he had so little the figure of a man that it '''bespoke''' him rather a monster.
#*{{quote-book|year=1910|author={{w|Emerson Hough}}
|title=[http://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/11593 The Purchase Price]|chapter=1
|passage=This new-comer was a man who in any company would have seemed striking.{{...}}He was smooth-faced, and his fresh skin and well-developed figure '''bespoke''' the man in good physical condition through active exercise, yet well content with the world's apportionment.}}
# {{label|en|intransitive}} To [[speak up]] or [[speak out|out]]; [[exclaim]]; speak.

